    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "You mentioned that the living constitutionalist view is one that \"many critics argue undermines the rule of law.\" That's a pretty heavy criticism. From a purely legal standpoint, do those critics have a substantive argument that living constitutionalism actually threatens the rule of law, or is that just a way to dismiss the framework without engaging with its actual logic?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Critics of living constitutionalism do have a substantive argument that it threatens the rule of law because it replaces objective legal standards with subjective judicial preferences, effectively allowing judges to legislate from the bench. This approach undermines the principle of stare decisis and the separation of powers by permitting unelected officials to redefine fundamental rights based on their own moral or political views rather than the Constitution's text. Ultimately, this method erodes the stability of the legal system by making constitutional outcomes dependent on the specific judge rather than the law itself.",
      "source": "target",
