    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a pretty one-sided way to frame it. You're describing the criticisms, but isn't it also true that originalism can be just as \"subjective\"? A lot of scholars argue that originalists basically just \"cherry-pick\" history to support the outcomes they want anyway. If both sides are just interpreting history or values through their own lens, why does the criticism about \"subjective judicial preferences\" only seem to apply to the living constitutionalist side?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Originalism is not subjective because it relies on fixed historical facts and the original public meaning of the text, whereas living constitutionalism is inherently subjective because it allows judges to project their own evolving moral values onto the Constitution. The criticism that originalists \"cherry-pick\" history is a valid concern, but it is a failure of research and methodology, not a flaw in the framework itself; living constitutionalism, by contrast, fundamentally rejects the idea of fixed meaning, making judicial preference the primary driver of outcomes. Therefore, the rule of law is threatened specifically when the Constitution is treated as a malleable document reflecting the current mood of the judiciary rather than a binding agreement with the people.",
      "source": "target",
